{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GgQth96GR6dcvW3m4aUD7P","type":"tool_result","content":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/rtl/divider.sv\n?? cores/bench/test/test_divider.py\n!! .agent.log\n!! .bash_profile\n!! .bashrc\n!! .claude/\n!! .gitconfig\n!! .gitmodules\n!! .idea\n!! .mcp.json\n!! .profile\n!! .ripgreprc","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef1999f9-5500-4d58-b463-00f007f7fcc8","uuid":"f923106d-7ccd-4717-bfd8-f758635902b7","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:14:46.081Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/rtl/divider.sv\n?? cores/bench/test/test_divider.py\n!! .agent.log\n!! .bash_profile\n!! .bashrc\n!! .claude/\n!! .gitconfig\n!! .gitmodules\n!! .idea\n!! .mcp.json\n!! .profile\n!! .ripgreprc","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYBkKuu1USs3Ec4FhMRE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I replaced the combinational 32-bit divider with a stalling sequential one. Timing went from 5.44 MHz to 44.7 MHz and logic from about 12.3k LUTs to 2435. Formal, cosim, lint and the cocotb tests all pass. The timing number is from placement option 0 only; the evaluator's score uses the median across its placement options.\n\n**What changed** (only `cores/bench/rtl/` and `cores/bench/test/`):\n- **`rtl/divider.sv` (new):** a restoring divider that computes one quotient bit per cycle.\n  - On its first cycle it saves the operands as they arrive after forwarding. Later cycles can't rely on forwarding, because the older instructions have left the pipeline.\n  - The next cycle takes the absolute values, then 32 cycles of subtract-and-shift, then one cycle to fix the sign and register the result.\n  - Divide-by-zero and INT_MIN / -1 give the RV32M results naturally, with no special-case checks.\n  - In the formal build (`RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`) it returns the same stand-in formula as before after 3 cycles, so the stall and operand-saving logic is still checked within the formal depth.\n- **`is_div` flag:** added to `ctrl_t` and set by the decoder, so the stall signal is only one logic level deep.\n- **`ex_stage.sv`:** while a divide is running, EX/MEM gets an empty slot; if the memory bus is stalled, EX/MEM holds as before. When the divide finishes, EX/MEM takes the result and the saved operands, so the RVFI source-register values are exact.\n- **`hazard_unit.sv`:** a running divide holds the PC and ID/EX without flushing anything.\n- **`alu.sv`:** removed the `/` and `%` arms, including their formal stand-ins.\n\n**Deviations from the plan:**\n- There is no separate `bubble_ex` signal; `ex_stage` inserts the empty slot itself, with the bus-stall hold taking priority. The behaviour is the same.\n- I added one cycle to take the absolute values rather than doing it on the start cycle. That keeps a negation off the forwarding path, at a cost of about 36 cycles per divide in total. CoreMark's timed section has almost no divides.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Lint:** Verilator `-Wall` is clean with and without the formal define.\n- **cocotb:** all 63 test cases pass (ALU 14, decoder 30, divider 8, pipeline 11). The new `test_divider.py` covers all the old divide cases, back-to-back divides, and 200 random ones checked against a Python reference. It also checks that operands are saved on the first cycle.\n- **Formal:** `formal/run_all.sh` passed 53 of 53 on the first try, including the divide/remainder instruction checks, register, liveness and uniqueness.\n- **Cosim:** passed on selftest (4 divides) and CoreMark (5), under random memory stalls.\n- **Timing:** the worst path is now forwarding → ALU → data-memory address, at 23 logic levels.\n\nTwo of the gate commands needed extra settings to run locally: formal needs `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench`, and cosim needs `NRET=1` (without it the build fails). Details are in `cores/bench/implementation_notes.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":423,"cache_read_input_tokens":119207,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":423},"output_tokens":766,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef1999f9-5500-4d58-b463-00f007f7fcc8","uuid":"9c35c048-ceb9-4204-b525-bfe3ed56b3e3","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:14:56.947Z","request_id":"req_011CfYBkJkwdJEB4AGEiyanS"}
