{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYDFACfk62qV1toFGdTK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've implemented the valid-bit squash change, and it passes lint, formal, cosim and the unit tests. Gowin timing reports **66.6 MHz**, up from the champion's 48.3 MHz. That number is from place option 0 only; the evaluator scores the median over three options, which I didn't run.\n\n**What changed** (all in `cores/bench/`):\n- **Fetch (`if_stage.sv`)**: the NOP mux is gone. The raw fetched word goes to decode, and the slot counts as valid whenever imem delivered.\n- **Decode (`id_stage.sv`)**: the ID/EX data fields have no reset or flush. They load whenever there is no dmem or MDU stall. A branch/jump redirect or a load-use now only clears a separate ID/EX valid bit.\n- **Hazard unit (`hazard_unit.sv`)**: load-use compares against the raw fetched word and only fires when ID/EX is valid. The old flush signals are replaced by a hold signal (ID/EX enable) and a squash signal (valid bit only).\n- **Execute (`ex_stage.sv`)**: redirect requires a valid instruction. Register-write, memory-read, memory-write, trap and MDU flags are cleared for invalid slots when they enter EX/MEM. The multi-cycle multiply/divide unit's stall bubble now just clears valid and those flags instead of zeroing the whole register.\n- **Dedicated memory-address adder** (`ex_stage.sv`, `mem_stage.sv`, `core_pkg.sv`): a new `mem_addr = rs1 + imm` field drives the dmem address, byte mask, misalignment check and load shift.\n\n**Deviations from the plan:**\n- I skipped the optional step 5 (registering the forward-mux selects a cycle early) to stay within the time budget.\n- I didn't add the extra valid gating on the dmem write/read enables in `mem_stage.sv`. The memory flags are already cleared for invalid slots, so it would only add logic.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Lint:** Verilator `-Wall` is clean.\n- **Formal:** 53 passed, 0 failed, first attempt. The command in the instructions (`bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg`) forces the two-channel wrapper and fails on this single-channel core. Running it without the config argument auto-detects the right one.\n- **Cosim:** passes (selftest and CoreMark with random stalls). It needs `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`; without `NRET=1` the build fails for the same reason.\n- **Unit tests:** all pass, including 5 new pipeline tests: wrong-path store/jump/multiply/load behind taken branches, load-use at a branch target, load-use under random imem and dmem stalls, a wrong-path divide before a real multiply, and back-to-back branches behind a stalled load. Two of these failed at first, but the fault was in the stall model I added to the test harness, not in the RTL, and they pass after fixing it.\n\n**Timing:** the old slowest path (redirect → NOP mux → regfile address / flush) is gone. The worst paths are now:\n- regfile → forward mux → ALU → EX/MEM, 20.31 ns\n- regfile → forward mux → branch compare → redirect → PC, 20.29 ns\n\nStep 5, or taking the jump/multiply-divide result mux off the ALU result capture, would be the next thing to try.\n\n**Concern:** the ID/EX and EX/MEM data registers no longer reset. Every consumer is gated by a valid bit or a control bit that does reset, and formal passes. A 4-state simulator could still show X on RVFI data fields of slots that never retire.\n\nThe notes are in `cores/bench/implementation_notes.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1492,"cache_read_input_tokens":133020,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1492},"output_tokens":792,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ec48193-9760-431e-8182-33e571c58677","uuid":"6d268fc8-e9be-4504-aa48-11a11ecb7e39","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:34:36.069Z","request_id":"req_011CfYDF9dSc7eY8xvRxbBc1"}
