{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYBiw6B6yZkRAXc1XvTg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've added the fetch-stage branch predictor to the bench core. It passes lint, formal, cosim and all unit tests, and CoreMark now takes about 6.6% fewer cycles than the hypothesis's baseline figure.\n\n**Results**\n- **Formal:** 53 passed, 0 failed on the first attempt, including pc_fwd, pc_bwd, unique, liveness, ill and cover.\n- **Cosim:** passed on both selftest and CoreMark. A plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` fails to compile because it builds for two retire channels and this core has one; it runs with `NRET=1` set. The orchestrator's eval sets this itself.\n- **CoreMark** (random stalls on both buses, as the evaluator runs it): CRCs correct and \"Correct operation validated\" printed. It took 4,193,149 cycles between the markers, about 419k per iteration against the hypothesis's figure of about 449k (it estimated 6.7% fewer).\n- **Unit tests:** all pass, including the full-pipeline test (11 cases) and the new predictor test (8 cases).\n- **Not run:** `make timing`, so the Fmax effect is unmeasured. The new paths into the PC mux should be much shorter than the ~200 ns divider path.\n\n**What changed** (all under `cores/bench/`)\n- **`rtl/branch_predictor.sv` (new):** looks at the raw fetched word, spots conditional branches and JAL, and computes the target as pc + offset.\n  - JAL is always predicted taken; a branch follows a table of 256 two-bit counters, reset to weakly-taken.\n  - Nothing is predicted when the target would be misaligned, and JALR is never predicted.\n- **`rtl/if_stage.sv`:** the next PC is a redirect if there is one, otherwise hold on a stall, otherwise the predicted target, otherwise pc+4. The prediction bit is cleared whenever the fetched instruction is invalid.\n- **`rtl/core_pkg.sv`, `rtl/id_stage.sv`:** the prediction bit is carried through to the execute stage and cleared on reset or flush.\n- **`rtl/ex_stage.sv`:** execute now redirects only when the actual direction differs from the prediction, or for a taken JALR. It also updates the counter table once per branch, not while a data-memory stall holds it. The retired next-PC and the misalignment trap are unchanged.\n- **`rtl/core.sv`:** connects the table update from execute back to fetch; no port changes.\n- **Tests:** added `test/test_branch_predictor.py`, and added the new source file to `test/test_pipeline.py`, which wouldn't build without it.\n\n**Deviations from the plan**\n- **Table storage:** I built the table from reset flops rather than distributed memory, because that memory can't be reset to weakly-taken. It adds 512 flip-flops and two read multiplexers.\n- **Illegal branch encodings:** the predictor doesn't filter them out. If one is predicted taken, execute redirects back to pc+4, which is correct but costs one cycle.\n\nDetails and the correctness argument are in `cores/bench/implementation_notes.md` (gitignored)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":853,"cache_read_input_tokens":101510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":853},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d9bb7652-2e74-41e3-a4f7-66b9970660e8","uuid":"6c30b486-2dcb-4e83-afac-a31cd60392d0","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:14:37.697Z","request_id":"req_011CfYBivUiL97F2wKPL52ku"}
