{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01Nc5bRhRenwMYKE1MepAYkF", "type": "tool_result", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Move MUL/DIV/REM out of the 1-cycle EX ALU into a multi-cycle stalling M-unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline runs at 5.44 MHz: the worst path (199.8 ns, 156 logic cells) is\n  MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> EX operand mux -> alu.sv -> EX/MEM (-> dmem BSRAM\n  address register). That depth is the fully combinational 32-bit `/` and `%`\n  (signed + unsigned, i.e. four ripple-array dividers) sitting in the single-cycle\n  EX stage, plus three separate 64-bit multipliers (ss/uu/su, 6 DSPs). Everything\n  else in the 5-stage pipe (forward mux, 32-bit add/compare, redirect) is ~10-15\n  LUT levels, so the pipeline is ~10x slower than it has to be because of\n  instructions that are nearly absent from the CoreMark timed loop: essentially no\n  DIV/REM, and ~1.6k MULs per iteration (N=9 matrix kernels: 729*2 + 81*4) against\n  ~449k cycles/iteration. Taking M-ext arithmetic off the single-cycle path costs\n  well under 1% IPC and should buy an order of magnitude in Fmax. It also removes\n  most of the 12.3k LUT4s (the dividers dominate area).\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure EX into a 1-cycle integer ALU plus a separate multi-cycle M-unit\n  (rtl/mdu.sv) with a start/busy/done handshake that stalls the front of the pipe.\n\n  1. alu.sv keeps only the RV32I ops (ADD..LUI). The M-ext arms (both the real\n     and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch) move into mdu.sv. No `/`, `%` or `*`\n     remains on the EX combinational path.\n\n  2. mdu.sv (new):\n     - Launch: when ID/EX holds a valid M-op and the unit is idle, latch op and\n       the POST-FORWARD rs1/rs2 values (the forwarding sources are correct in\n       this cycle exactly as for a normal ALU op).\n     - Multiply: one shared 33x33 signed multiplier (operands sign- or\n       zero-extended per MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU) replacing the three 64-bit\n       products; input registers -> product register (maps onto Gowin DSP\n       internal pipeline regs), then low/high-half select. ~3-cycle latency.\n     - Divide: radix-2 restoring divider on operand magnitudes, 1 quotient bit\n       per cycle (32 iterations) + sign fix-up; RV32M special cases handled at\n       launch as an early-out (div-by-0 -> q=all-ones, r=dividend;\n       INT_MIN/-1 -> q=INT_MIN, r=0). The iteration datapath is a single 33-bit\n       subtract + shift register, i.e. one short carry chain per cycle.\n     - Under `ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` the unit uses the same handshake but\n       produces the existing ALTOPS formulas with a short fixed latency (1-2\n       cycles), so riscv-formal still exercises the stall/launch/complete\n       control within its 20-step BMC depth while real arithmetic stays out of\n       the solver.\n     - done/result are held until EX/MEM actually captures them (a dmem stall\n       may freeze EX/MEM in the completion cycle).\n\n  3. Pipeline control (hazard_unit.sv / core.sv / ex_stage.sv):\n     - mdu_stall = ID/EX is a valid M-op && !mdu_done. While asserted:\n       stall_if = stall_id = 1 (PC and ID/EX hold, no flush), and EX/MEM\n       captures a bubble (valid=0, reg_write=0, mem_read/mem_write=0) so older\n       instructions drain through MEM/WB normally. dmem_stall keeps priority\n       (EX/MEM holds). No interaction with redirect (an M-op is never a\n       branch/jump) or load-use (ID/EX is not a load).\n     - On completion EX/MEM captures the M-op with alu_result = mdu result, and\n       rs1_val/rs2_val/write_data taken from the MDU's LATCHED operands, not the\n       live forward mux (while waiting, the producer may have retired to the\n       regfile, making ID/EX.rs?_val stale and the live mux wrong for RVFI).\n     - After completion the M-op result forwards from EX/MEM/MEM-WB exactly\n       like any ALU result; no forwarding-unit change needed.\n\n  4. Tests: add test/test_mdu.py (clocked cocotb) carrying every M-ext vector", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "37edf622-3c17-4b0b-a289-58487f1171a6", "uuid": "8d041d81-603a-4d18-85a3-b008db3a8378", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:22:33.051Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "id: hyp-20260929-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Move MUL/DIV/REM out of the 1-cycle EX ALU into a multi-cycle stalling M-unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline runs at 5.44 MHz: the worst path (199.8 ns, 156 logic cells) is\n  MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> EX operand mux -> alu.sv -> EX/MEM (-> dmem BSRAM\n  address register). That depth is the fully combinational 32-bit `/` and `%`\n  (signed + unsigned, i.e. four ripple-array dividers) sitting in the single-cycle\n  EX stage, plus three separate 64-bit multipliers (ss/uu/su, 6 DSPs). Everything\n  else in the 5-stage pipe (forward mux, 32-bit add/compare, redirect) is ~10-15\n  LUT levels, so the pipeline is ~10x slower than it has to be because of\n  instructions that are nearly absent from the CoreMark timed loop: essentially no\n  DIV/REM, and ~1.6k MULs per iteration (N=9 matrix kernels: 729*2 + 81*4) against\n  ~449k cycles/iteration. Taking M-ext arithmetic off the single-cycle path costs\n  well under 1% IPC and should buy an order of magnitude in Fmax. It also removes\n  most of the 12.3k LUT4s (the dividers dominate area).\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure EX into a 1-cycle integer ALU plus a separate multi-cycle M-unit\n  (rtl/mdu.sv) with a start/busy/done handshake that stalls the front of the pipe.\n\n  1. alu.sv keeps only the RV32I ops (ADD..LUI). The M-ext arms (both the real\n     and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch) move into mdu.sv. No `/`, `%` or `*`\n     remains on the EX combinational path.\n\n  2. mdu.sv (new):\n     - Launch: when ID/EX holds a valid M-op and the unit is idle, latch op and\n       the POST-FORWARD rs1/rs2 values (the forwarding sources are correct in\n       this cycle exactly as for a normal A