{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H2AEJc98R54CdcWTbjsv7V","type":"tool_result","content":"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (regression, +739.85%): Making only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU a stalling radix-2 sequential unit took Fmax from ~5.4 to ~45 MHz at negligible CoreMark IPC cost, since the combinational `/`/`%` was nearly the whole critical path; it is labelled a regression only because sibling r1s1 also moved MUL into the multi-cycle unit and scored ~1.4% higher, which is inside the ~6% seed spread, so the DSP multiplier is not a significant next Fmax limiter.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (improvement, +752.15%): Once DIV/REM and MUL are both in a stalling M-unit, Fmax stops near the ~49 MHz probe ceiling set by the regfile LUTRAM -> EX branch compare -> redirect -> hazard -> ID/EX flush path (~25.6 ns, 17 levels), so moving MUL off EX adds almost nothing; later Fmax work has to shorten redirect/flush, and new stall signals such as mdu_stall must come only from registered ID/EX + done-flop state so they do not lengthen that path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.60%): A fetch-stage predictor (predecoded pc+imm target, 256-entry bimodal BHT, always-taken JAL, EX redirects only on a direction mismatch) gained +6.6% from IPC alone, close to the +7.1% the reference.py trace model predicted, so that model is a reliable IPC estimator; it counts as a regression only because the r1s0/r1s1 siblings that moved the divider off EX scored ~8x higher, and porting it to that ~49 MHz champion must keep the `actual_taken != pred_taken` compare and the pc+4 redirect-target mux from lengthening the now-critical EX branch compare -> redirect -> flush path.\n---\n3 cores/bench/LESSONS.md","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a82b8145-33ef-4391-a07a-4bb9a5329cef","uuid":"fdbc9f4f-3d5c-4cf7-98b6-abe40b6030a5","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:35:48.709Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-