{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_0119nCpbPLxDorNCTQMJUvak", "type": "tool_result", "content": "- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (regression, +739.85%): Making only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU a stalling radix-2 sequential unit took Fmax from ~5.4 to ~45 MHz at negligible CoreMark IPC cost, since the combinational `/`/`%` was nearly the whole critical path; it is labelled a regression only because sibling r1s1 also moved MUL into the multi-cycle unit and scored ~1.4% higher, which is inside the ~6% seed spread, so the DSP multiplier is not a significant next Fmax limiter.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (improvement, +752.15%): Once DIV/REM and MUL are both in a stalling M-unit, Fmax stops near the ~49 MHz probe ceiling set by the regfile LUTRAM -> EX branch compare -> redirect -> hazard -> ID/EX flush path (~25.6 ns, 17 levels), so moving MUL off EX adds almost nothing; later Fmax work has to shorten redirect/flush, and new stall signals such as mdu_stall must come only from registered ID/EX + done-flop state so they do not lengthen that path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.60%): A fetch-stage predictor (predecoded pc+imm target, 256-entry bimodal BHT, always-taken JAL, EX redirects only on a direction mismatch) gained +6.6% from IPC alone, close to the +7.1% the reference.py trace model predicted, so that model is a reliable IPC estimator; it counts as a regression only because the r1s0/r1s1 siblings that moved the divider off EX scored ~8x higher, and porting it to that ~49 MHz champion must keep the `actual_taken != pred_taken` compare and the pc+4 redirect-target mux from lengthening the now-critical EX branch compare -> redirect -> flush path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r2s1 (improvement, +37.69%): Squashing wrong-path/load-use slots by clearing only the ID/EX valid bit (raw imem word into the decoder, regfile read addresses and load-use compare; no IF NOP mux, no flush_id sync-reset, and the ID/EX CE driven only by dmem/mdu stall; side effects valid-gated at EX/MEM capture) took the EX redirect off the regfile-read/hazard/high-fanout CE path, giving roughly the predicted ~66 MHz at the same cycle count, so flush-by-zeroing data should be avoided in this pipeline; the gain is shared with a dedicated rs1+imm dmem-address adder added in the same change, so the two effects were not measured separately.\nCORE_PHILOSOPHY.md\ncore.yaml\nexperiments\ngenerated\nLESSONS.md\nobj_dir\nrtl\ntest\nworktrees", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f1f121a6-b80e-457a-be4c-5b6a93906dd8", "uuid": "5ba173cc-23f4-4f22-8500-1578996dde65", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:36:04.453Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (regression, +739.85%): Making only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU a stalling radix-2 sequential unit took Fmax from ~5.4 to ~45 MHz at negligible CoreMark IPC cost, since the combinational `/`/`%` was nearly the whole critical path; it is labelled a regression only because sibling r1s1 also moved MUL into the multi-cycle unit and scored ~1.4% higher, which is inside the ~6% seed spread, so the DSP multiplier is not a significant next Fmax limiter.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (improvement, +752.15%): Once DIV/REM and MUL are both in a stalling M-unit, Fmax stops near the ~49 MHz probe ceiling set by the regfile LUTRAM -> EX branch compare -> redirect -> hazard -> ID/EX flush path (~25.6 ns, 17 levels), so moving MUL off EX adds almost nothing; later Fmax work has to shorten redirect/flush, and new stall signals such as mdu_stall must come only from registered ID/EX + done-flop state so they do not lengthen that path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.60%): A fetch-stage predictor (predecoded pc+imm target, 256-entry bimodal BHT, always-taken JAL, EX redirects only on a direction mismatch) gained +6.6% from IPC alone, close to the +7.1% the reference.py trace model predicted, so that model is a reliable IPC estimator; it counts as a regression only because the r1s0/r1s1 siblings that moved the divider off EX scored ~8x higher, and porting it to that ~49 MHz champion must keep the `actual_taken != pred_taken` compare and the pc+4 redirect-target mux from lengthening the now-critical EX branch compare -> redirect -> flush path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r2s1 (improvement, +37.69%): Squashing wrong-path/load-use slots by clearing only the ID/EX valid bit (raw imem word into the decoder, regfile read addresses and load-use compare; no IF NOP mux, no flush_id sync-reset, and the ID/EX CE driven only by dmem/mdu stall; side effects valid-gated at EX/MEM capture) took the EX redirect off the regfile-read/hazard/high-fanout CE path, giving roughly the predicted ~66 MHz at the same cycle count, so flush-by-zeroing data should be avoided in this pipeline; the gain is shared with a dedicated rs1+imm dmem-address adder added in the same change, so the two effects were not measured separately.\nCORE_PHILOSOPHY.md\ncore.yaml\nexperiments\ngenerated\nLESSONS.md\nobj_dir\nrtl\ntest\nworktrees", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
