{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WCo8Jz4GFprvUKRNirv88P","type":"tool_result","content":"00000000: 0a                                       .\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r2s1 (improvement, +37.69%): Squashing wrong-path/load-use slots by clearing only the ID/EX valid bit (raw imem word into the decoder, regfile read addresses and load-use compare; no IF NOP mux, no flush_id sync-reset, and the ID/EX CE driven only by dmem/mdu stall; side effects valid-gated at EX/MEM capture) took the EX redirect off the regfile-read/hazard/high-fanout CE path, giving roughly the predicted ~66 MHz at the same cycle count, so flush-by-zeroing data should be avoided in this pipeline; the gain is shared with a dedicated rs1+imm dmem-address adder added in the same change, so the two effects were not measured separately.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, +26.66%): Adding the r1s2 fetch predictor (predecode, pc+imm adder, 256x2b BHT) and acting on a registered EX/MEM.mispredict one cycle late did take the EX branch compare off the Fmax path, but it lost to sibling r2s1's valid-bit squash (+37.69%, 66.6 MHz at unchanged cycles); since fitness = 10*Fmax/Mcycles and cycles must fall between r1s2's 4.19M and the champion's 4.68M, r2s2 can only have reached ~55-61 MHz, so the new fetch predecode -> pc+imm -> next_pc path and/or the flush_id zeroing of ID/EX on load-use it kept now caps Fmax well below r2s1, and the ~5% IPC from prediction does not make up for that; any predictor added on top of r2s1 must keep its fetch -> PC path shorter than r2s1's ~15 ns EX -> ALU -> EX/MEM path.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1f121a6-b80e-457a-be4c-5b6a93906dd8","uuid":"3eeb2ef5-175e-476b-b5ed-8a97792684c1","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:36:41.085Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"00000000: 0a                                       .\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r2s1 (improvement, +37.69%): Squashing wrong-path/load-use slots by clearing only the ID/EX valid bit (raw imem word into the decoder, regfile read addresses and load-use compare; no IF NOP mux, no flush_id sync-reset, and the ID/EX CE driven only by dmem/mdu stall; side effects valid-gated at EX/MEM capture) took the EX redirect off the regfile-read/hazard/high-fanout CE path, giving roughly the predicted ~66 MHz at the same cycle count, so flush-by-zeroing data should be avoided in this pipeline; the gain is shared with a dedicated rs1+imm dmem-address adder added in the same change, so the two effects were not measured separately.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, +26.66%): Adding the r1s2 fetch predictor (predecode, pc+imm adder, 256x2b BHT) and acting on a registered EX/MEM.mispredict one cycle late did take the EX branch compare off the Fmax path, but it lost to sibling r2s1's valid-bit squash (+37.69%, 66.6 MHz at unchanged cycles); since fitness = 10*Fmax/Mcycles and cycles must fall between r1s2's 4.19M and the champion's 4.68M, r2s2 can only have reached ~55-61 MHz, so the new fetch predecode -> pc+imm -> next_pc path and/or the flush_id zeroing of ID/EX on load-use it kept now caps Fmax well below r2s1, and the ~5% IPC from prediction does not make up for that; any predictor added on top of r2s1 must keep its fetch -> PC path shorter than r2s1's ~15 ns EX -> ALU -> EX/MEM path.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYDRFRUhWtBuCDYwfqYo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added one lesson bullet for hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 to the end of `cores/bench/LESSONS.md`. No other file was changed.\n\nThis run beat the champion by 26.66% but is labelled a regression because the other same-round run (r2s1) scored higher: +37.69%, at 66.6 MHz with the same cycle count. Both runs took the EX branch-compare → redirect chain off the critical path. r2s1 did it with a valid-bit squash and no predictor. This run used a fetch predictor plus a mispredict redirect taken from a register one cycle late.\n\nThe Fmax and cycle numbers for this run weren't logged yet, so the lesson's clock-speed claim is worked out from the score rather than measured:\n- **Score formula:** fitness = 10 × Fmax ÷ cycles in millions. This matches the r1s1, r1s2 and r2s1 records exactly.\n- **Cycle range:** this run's cycles must fall between r1s2's 4.19M and the champion's 4.68M.\n- **Implied clock:** a score of 130.81 then means about 55–61 MHz, clearly below r2s1's 66.6 MHz.\n\nThe lesson names two likely limiters without choosing between them, since there's no critical-path report:\n- the new fetch path (predecode → pc+imm adder → next PC);\n- the load-use `flush_id` that still zeroes the ID/EX register, which the r2s1 lesson says to avoid.\n\nIt ends with the constraint for next time: a predictor added on top of r2s1 needs its fetch → PC path to be shorter than r2s1's ~15 ns EX → ALU → EX/MEM path."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2732,"cache_read_input_tokens":33695,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2732},"output_tokens":555,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_