{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfYDXBsqAjpevBawyK7mu", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": "\n…[29284 of 32356 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2848adb55b9c3928]…\"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7761, "cache_read_input_tokens": 55602, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7761}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "95ca47cb-311b-4a9c-a070-d04a443baf2a", "uuid": "184dea3a-354f-47dc-a248-f32335dad116", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:39:44.040Z", "request_id": "req_011CfYDXAzEvNfd5PJD4b9RT"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfYDXBsqAjpevBawyK7mu", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01JQZMAe3b4gQwTE9TjaX7F5", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-001-r3s0\ntitle: \"Pull the regfile LUTRAM read out of EX (keep real ID/EX operand flops) and pre-register forwarding matches\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion (r2s1, 66.56 MHz, 142.21 iter/s) is limited by one path:\n  from cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s (the regfile LUTRAM) through u_id and the\n  u_ex forward mux and ALU to cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0. That is 20.3 ns\n  arrival over 14 cells. The path has no flop between the regfile RAM and\n  the EX/MEM register. So the ID/EX rs1_val/rs2_val registers no longer\n  exist as flops. In r1s1 the same kind of path ended at\n  cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_*, so the flop was there.\n  The cause is r2s1's valid-bit squash. It removed the flush_id sync-reset\n  from the ID/EX data flops, which left them as plain CE registers. The\n  regfile read feeding those registers (x0 zero plus write-first bypass)\n  is the textbook \"transparent synchronous read\" pattern. GowinSynthesis\n  therefore folded rs1_val/rs2_val into the memory as a registered-address\n  LUTRAM, which moves the whole LUTRAM read (RAM16SDP + MUX2_LUT5 +\n  routing, about 3 ns) into the EX cycle. The ID stage does very little\n  in the same cycle: in the timed netlist, imem data comes from an LFSR\n  flop, then 5-bit address, LUTRAM, and the flop. So the ID cycle has\n  lots of slack and the EX cycle pays for the RAM.\n  The second-largest part of that path is the forward-select compare\n  (id_ex.rs?_addr == ex_mem.rd / mem_wb.rd, rd != 0, reg_write). It is\n  about 2 LUT levels plus routing, and it arrives at the operand-mux\n  select after the data does.\n  Both costs sit in front of the ALU, the branch comparator (redirect\n  path) and the dmem address adder. So they cap every EX path together.\nhypothesis: |\n  Two coupled micro-optimizations that shorten the EX operand path. IPC\n  does not change (same cycle count, 4.68M).\n\n  1. Keep real ID/EX operand flops so the LUTRAM read stays in ID.\n     In id_stage.sv, give reg_q.rs1_val / reg_q.rs2_val a synchronous\n     reset on the global `reset`:\n     `if (reset) {rs1_val,rs2_val} <= '0; else if (!hold) ...`.\n     Keep the rest of ID/EX CE-only, and keep the valid-bit squash exactly\n     as it is. `reset` is a port/registered signal, and Gowin DFFRE\n     provides sync reset plus CE for free. r1s1 is evidence that a\n     sync-reset on these flops blocks the absorption: its flush_id reset\n     kept them as flops. Also add `/* synthesis syn_preserve = 1 */` (or\n     `(* syn_preserve = 1 *)`) on those two fields.\n     The EX path then starts at a flop instead of a RAM read. The ID path\n     (imem, then LUTRAM, then x0/bypass mux, then flop) is short.\n     Verify with `make timing TARGET=bench` that the worst path no longer\n     starts at cpu/u_rf/regs_* and ends in cpu/u_ex. If Gowin still\n     absorbs the flops, move the x0 and write-first bypass mux out of\n     reg_file.sv and into id_stage.sv just before the flop, and combine\n     that with the reset.\n\n  2. Pre-register forwarding address matches.\n     When ID/EX captures (!hold), next cycle's EX/MEM is exactly the\n     current ID/EX and next cycle's MEM/WB is exactly the current EX/MEM.\n     This holds because !hold implies no dmem_stall and no mdu_stall, so\n     both registers advance.\n     So in ID compute, for s in {rs1, rs2} with rs = in.instr[19:15] /\n     [24:20]:\n       m_s_exmem_next = (id_ex.rd == rs) && (id_ex.rd != 0)\n       m_s_memwb_next = (ex_mem.rd == rs) && (ex_mem.rd != 0)\n     Store these four flags in new id_ex_t fields, captured under the same\n     !hold CE.\n     In EX, the forward selects become:\n       fwd_s = (m_s_exmem_q && ex_mem.ctrl.reg_write) ? EXMEM\n             : (m_s_memwb_q && mem_wb.ctrl.reg_write) ? MEMWB : RF\n     The reg_write qualifiers stay as live registered bits because\n     EX/MEM.reg_write already includes the valid, mdu-bubble and\n     misaligned-jump gating. With this, each operand-mux select is one\n     LUT on flop outputs instead of a 5-bit compare tree followed by\n     priority logic.\n     Why this is safe:\n       - dmem stall: EX/MEM, ID/EX and MEM/WB data (incl. rd/reg_write)\n         are all frozen, so the snapshot stays exact.\n       - mdu stall: EX/MEM takes bubbles while the M-op sits in ID/EX.\n         The MDU latches its post-forward operands in the first cycle,\n         when the snapshot is exact. rs?_ret, the RVFI values and the\n         store data already come from the MDU latches, and an M-op is\n         never a branch or memory op.\n       - load-use bubble / redirect / imem stall: the slot is invalid,\n         and the re-presented consumer recomputes its flags against the\n         then-current ID/EX / EX/MEM.\n       - reset: EX/MEM ctrl_q and MEM/WB reset to reg_write=0.\n\n  3. (Cheap, optional) Fold `is_jump ? pc+4 : is_mdu ? mdu_result :\n     alu_result` into one AND-OR stage. Take pc+4 (link) from a\n     registered ID/EX field computed in ID. This removes one more LUT\n     level after the ALU on the EX -> EX/MEM.alu_result endpoint.\n\n  Expected result: EX critical path drops from about 15.0 ns effective\n  (66.6 MHz) to about 12 ns (~80-85 MHz) at unchanged cycle count and\n  roughly unchanged LUTs. That is about +20% fitness. The next limiter\n  should be the forward-mux, branch compare, redirect, PC/valid path, or\n  the ALU carry/shift path itself.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 18\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Sync-reset (global reset) plus syn_preserve on the ID/EX rs1_val/rs2_val flops, so GowinSynthesis cannot absorb them into a registered-address LUTRAM and the regfile read stays in ID. New inputs id_ex_rd and ex_mem_rd. Compute and register the four forwarding match flags (rs1/rs2 vs next-cycle EX/MEM rd and MEM/WB rd, rd!=0) under the existing !hold CE. Optionally register link = pc+4.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add to id_ex_t: fwd_rs1_exmem, fwd_rs1_memwb, fwd_rs2_exmem, fwd_rs2_memwb (1 bit each), and optionally link [31:0] (pc+4).\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the live 5-bit address compares with the registered match flags, qualified only by ex_mem.ctrl.reg_write and mem_wb.ctrl.reg_write. Priority stays EX/MEM > MEM/WB > RF. Alternatively delete the module and do the qualification inline in ex_stage.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Operand muxes use the pre-registered selects. Optionally collapse the jump-link/MDU/ALU result selection into a single AND-OR stage using the registered link value.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire id_ex_w.rd and ex_mem_w.rd into id_stage for match-flag computation. Drive forward selects from the registered flags plus the EX/MEM and MEM/WB reg_write bits. No IO change.\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Only if needed (fallback): expose the raw async LUTRAM read and move the x0 and write-first bypass mux into id_stage in front of the reset-able operand flop, so the flop cannot be merged into the memory.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7761, "cache_read_input_tokens": 55602, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7761}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "95ca47cb-311b-4a9c-a070-d04a443baf2a", "uuid": "19b140d9-6d97-4a3e-84a9-0234ed2b7350", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:40:12.641Z", "request_id": "req_011CfYDXAzEvNfd5PJD4b9RT"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01JQZMAe3b4gQwTE9TjaX7F5", "type": "tool_result", "content": "File created successfully at: /srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0.yaml (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "95ca47cb-311b-4a9c-a070-d04a443baf2a", "uuid": "08872f54-cef9-427f-ab9b-3fde269d57f5", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:40:12.663Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "create", "filePath": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-001-r3s0\ntitle: \"Pull the regfile LUTRAM read out of EX (keep real ID/EX operand flops) and pre-register forwarding matches\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion (r2s1, 66.56 MHz, 142.21 iter/s) is limited by one path:\n  from cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s (the regfile LUTRAM) through u_id and the\n  u_ex forward mux and ALU to cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0. That is 20.3 ns\n  arrival over 14 cells. The path has no flop between the regfile RAM and\n  the EX/MEM register. So the ID/EX rs1_val/rs2_val registers no longer\n  exist as flops. In r1s1 the same kind of path ended at\n  cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_*, so the flop was there.\n  The cause is r2s1's valid-bit squash. It removed the flush_id sync-reset\n  from the ID/EX data flops, which left them as plain CE registers. The\n  regfile read feeding those registers (x0 zero plus write-first bypass)\n  is the textbook \"transparent synchronous read\" pattern. GowinSynthesis\n  therefore folded rs1_val/rs2_val into the memory as a registered-address\n  LUTRAM, which moves the whole LUTRAM read (RAM16SDP + MUX2_LUT5 +\n  routing, about 3 ns) into the EX cycle. The ID stage does very little\n  in the same cycle: in the timed netlist, imem data comes from an LFSR\n  flop, then 5-bit address, LUTRAM, and the flop. So the ID cycle has\n  lots of slack and the EX cycle pays for the RAM.\n  The second-largest part of that path is the forward-select compare\n  (id_ex.rs?_addr == ex_mem.rd / mem_wb.rd, rd != 0, reg_write). It is\n  about 2 LUT levels plus routing, and it arrives at the operand-mux\n  select after the data does.\n  Both costs sit in front of the ALU, the branch comparator (redirect\n  path) and the dmem address adder. So they cap every EX path together.\nhypothesis: |\n  Two coupled micro-optimizations that shorten the EX operand path. IPC\n  does not change (same cycle count, 4.68M).\n\n  1. Keep real ID/EX operand flops so the LUTRAM read stays in ID.\n     In id_stage.sv, give reg_q.rs1_val / reg_q.rs2_val a synchronous\n     reset on the global `reset`:\n     `if (reset) {rs1_val,rs2_val} <= '0; else if (!hold) ...`.\n     Keep the rest of ID/EX CE-only, and keep the valid-bit squash exactly\n     as it is. `reset` is a port/registered signal, and Gowin DFFRE\n     provides sync reset plus CE for free. r1s1 is evidence that a\n     sync-reset on these flops blocks the absorption: its flush_id reset\n     kept them as flops. Also add `/* synthesis syn_preserve = 1 */` (or\n     `(* syn_preserve = 1 *)`) on those two fields.\n     The EX path then starts at a flop instead of a RAM read. The ID path\n     (imem, then LUTRAM, then x0/bypass mux, then flop) is short.\n     Verify with `make timing TARGET=bench` that the worst path no longer\n     starts at cpu/u_rf/regs_* and ends in cpu/u_ex. If Gowin still\n     absorbs the flops, move the x0 and write-first bypass mux out of\n     reg_file.sv and into id_stage.sv just before the flop, and combine\n     that with the reset.\n\n  2. Pre-register forwarding address matches.\n     When ID/EX captures (!hold), next cycle's EX/MEM is exactly the\n     current ID/EX and next cycle's MEM/WB is exactly the current EX/MEM.\n     This holds because !hold implies no dmem_stall and no mdu_stall, so\n     both registers advance.\n     So in ID compute, for s in {rs1, rs2} with rs = in.instr[19:15] /\n     [24:20]:\n       m_s_exmem_next = (id_ex.rd == rs) && (id_ex.rd != 0)\n       m_s_memwb_next = (ex_mem.rd == rs) && (ex_mem.rd != 0)\n     Store these four flags in new id_ex_t fields, captured under the same\n     !hold CE.\n     In EX, the forward selects become:\n       fwd_s = (m_s_exmem_q && ex_mem.ctrl.reg_write) ? EXMEM\n             : (m_s_memwb_q && mem_wb.ctrl.reg_write) ? MEMWB : RF\n     The reg_write qualifiers stay as live registered bits because\n     EX/MEM.reg_write already includes the valid, mdu-bubble and\n     misaligned-jump gating. With this, each operand-mux select is one\n     LUT on flop outputs instead of a 5-bit compare tree followed by\n     priority logic.\n     Why this is safe:\n       - dmem stall: EX/MEM, ID/EX and MEM/WB data (incl. rd/reg_write)\n         are all frozen, so the snapshot stays exact.\n       - mdu stall: EX/MEM takes bubbles while the M-op sits in ID/EX.\n         The MDU latches its post-forward operands in the first cycle,\n         when the snapshot is exact. rs?_ret, the RVFI values and the\n         store data already come from the MDU latches, and an M-op is\n         never a branch or memory op.\n       - load-use bubble / redirect / imem stall: the slot is invalid,\n         and the re-presented consumer recomputes its flags against the\n         then-current ID/EX / EX/MEM.\n       - reset: EX/MEM ctrl_q and MEM/WB reset to reg_write=0.\n\n  3. (Cheap, optional) Fold `is_jump ? pc+4 : is_mdu ? mdu_result :\n     alu_result` into one AND-OR stage. Take pc+4 (link) from a\n     registered ID/EX field computed in ID. This removes one more LUT\n     level after the ALU on the EX -> EX/MEM.alu_result endpoint.\n\n  Expected result: EX critical path drops from about 15.0 ns effective\n  (66.6 MHz) to about 12 ns (~80-85 MHz) at unchanged cycle count and\n  roughly unchanged LUTs. That is about +20% fitness. The next limiter\n  should be the forward-mux, branch compare, redirect, PC/valid path, or\n  the ALU carry/shift path itself.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 18\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Sync-reset (global reset) plus syn_preserve on the ID/EX rs1_val/rs2_val flops, so GowinSynthesis cannot absorb them into a registered-address LUTRAM and the regfile read stays in ID. New inputs id_ex_rd and ex_mem_rd. Compute and register the four forwarding match flags (rs1/rs2 vs next-cycle EX/MEM rd and MEM/WB rd, rd!=0) under the existing !hold CE. Optionally register link = pc+4.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add to id_ex_t: fwd_rs1_exmem, fwd_rs1_memwb, fwd_rs2_exmem, fwd_rs2_memwb (1 bit each), and optionally link [31:0] (pc+4).\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the live 5-bit address compares with the registered match flags, qualified only by ex_mem.ctrl.reg_write and mem_wb.ctrl.reg_write. Priority stays EX/MEM > MEM/WB > RF. Alternatively delete the module and do the qualification inline in ex_stage.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Operand muxes use the pre-registered selects. Optionally collapse the jump-link/MDU/ALU result selection into a single AND-OR stage using the registered link value.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire id_ex_w.rd and ex_mem_w.rd into id_stage for match-flag computation. Drive forward selects from the registered flags plus the EX/MEM and MEM/WB reg_write bits. No IO change.\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Only if needed (fallback): expose the raw async LUTRAM read and move the x0 and write-first bypass mux into id_stage in front of the reset-able operand flop, so the flop cannot be merged into the memory.\"\n", "structuredPatch": [], "originalFile": null, "userModified": false}}
