{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYDh5E1Pcb5GYMiJDQd7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the hypothesis `hyp-20260929-001-r3s0` (micro_opt) to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r3s0.yaml`, and it passes the schema check. I did not run `make timing` to confirm the diagnosis below; I left it out to stay inside the time limit.\n\n**Why the champion is slow:** its worst path starts at the register file's LUT memory (`cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s`) and ends at the EX/MEM register (`u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0`). Nothing is clocked in between. In r1s1 the same kind of path ended at an ID/EX `rs1_val` flop. The likely cause is r2s1's valid-bit squash change: it removed the reset from the ID/EX operand flops, and synthesis seems to have folded those flops into the memory. That moves the whole register-file read (about 3 ns) into the EX cycle. Meanwhile the ID cycle has spare time, because in the timed netlist the instruction word comes straight from a flop.\n\n**Proposed changes:**\n1. **Keep real ID/EX operand flops.** Add a synchronous reset on the global `reset` and a `syn_preserve` attribute to `rs1_val`/`rs2_val`. A reset on these flops is what kept them as separate flops in r1s1, so there is evidence it blocks the folding. The implementer should check with `make timing` that the path no longer starts at the register file. The fallback is to move the x0 and write-first bypass mux into `id_stage` just before the flop.\n2. **Compute the forwarding matches one stage early.** When ID/EX loads, the next EX/MEM is exactly the current ID/EX and the next MEM/WB is exactly the current EX/MEM. So ID can compare the source registers against those destination registers and store four match bits. In EX, each operand-mux select then becomes one LUT: the stored bit ANDed with the live `reg_write` bit. I checked this by hand against data-memory stalls, multiply/divide stalls, load-use bubbles, redirects, instruction-fetch stalls and reset.\n3. **Optional:** register `pc+4` in ID and merge the jump-link / multiply-divide / ALU result selection into a single stage.\n\n**Expected effect:** the cycle count stays at 4.68M. The EX path should drop from about 15.0 ns to about 12 ns, which is roughly 80–85 MHz and about +18% fitness (medium confidence). That clears the 4.6% acceptance bar."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":293,"cache_read_input_tokens":76697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":293},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95ca47cb-311b-4a9c-a070-d04a443baf2a","uuid":"4d35a3df-d22b-4ac3-85e5-bb5764cfc9c3","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:40:25.799Z","request_id":"req_011CfYDh4kimNbyZvsbPJazk"}
