{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfYAyzDW3QyVqzX7jyFG9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01YA2D2271tW53sa2PAioCP6", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-002-r1s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r1s1.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Move MUL/DIV/REM out of the 1-cycle EX ALU into a multi-cycle stalling M-unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline runs at 5.44 MHz: the worst path (199.8 ns, 156 logic cells) is\n  MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> EX operand mux -> alu.sv -> EX/MEM (-> dmem BSRAM\n  address register). That depth is the fully combinational 32-bit `/` and `%`\n  (signed + unsigned, i.e. four ripple-array dividers) sitting in the single-cycle\n  EX stage, plus three separate 64-bit multipliers (ss/uu/su, 6 DSPs). Everything\n  else in the 5-stage pipe (forward mux, 32-bit add/compare, redirect) is ~10-15\n  LUT levels, so the pipeline is ~10x slower than it has to be because of\n  instructions that are nearly absent from the CoreMark timed loop: essentially no\n  DIV/REM, and ~1.6k MULs per iteration (N=9 matrix kernels: 729*2 + 81*4) against\n  ~449k cycles/iteration. Taking M-ext arithmetic off the single-cycle path costs\n  well under 1% IPC and should buy an order of magnitude in Fmax. It also removes\n  most of the 12.3k LUT4s (the dividers dominate area).\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure EX into a 1-cycle integer ALU plus a separate multi-cycle M-unit\n  (rtl/mdu.sv) with a start/busy/done handshake that stalls the front of the pipe.\n\n  1. alu.sv keeps only the RV32I ops (ADD..LUI). The M-ext arms (both the real\n     and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch) move into mdu.sv. No `/`, `%` or `*`\n     remains on the EX combinational path.\n\n  2. mdu.sv (new):\n     - Launch: when ID/EX holds a valid M-op and the unit is idle, latch op and\n       the POST-FORWARD rs1/rs2 values (the forwarding sources are correct in\n       this cycle exactly as for a normal ALU op).\n     - Multiply: one shared 33x33 signed multiplier (operands sign- or\n       zero-extended per MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU) replacing the three 64-bit\n       products; input registers -> product register (maps onto Gowin DSP\n       internal pipeline regs), then low/high-half select. ~3-cycle latency.\n     - Divide: radix-2 restoring divider on operand magnitudes, 1 quotient bit\n       per cycle (32 iterations) + sign fix-up; RV32M special cases handled at\n       launch as an early-out (div-by-0 -> q=all-ones, r=dividend;\n       INT_MIN/-1 -> q=INT_MIN, r=0). The iteration datapath is a single 33-bit\n       subtract + shift register, i.e. one short carry chain per cycle.\n     - Under `ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` the unit uses the same handshake but\n       produces the existing ALTOPS formulas with a short fixed latency (1-2\n       cycles), so riscv-formal still exercises the stall/launch/complete\n       control within its 20-step BMC depth while real arithmetic stays out of\n       the solver.\n     - done/result are held until EX/MEM actually captures them (a dmem stall\n       may freeze EX/MEM in the completion cycle).\n\n  3. Pipeline control (hazard_unit.sv / core.sv / ex_stage.sv):\n     - mdu_stall = ID/EX is a valid M-op && !mdu_done. While asserted:\n       stall_if = stall_id = 1 (PC and ID/EX hold, no flush), and EX/MEM\n       captures a bubble (valid=0, reg_write=0, mem_read/mem_write=0) so older\n       instructions drain through MEM/WB normally. dmem_stall keeps priority\n       (EX/MEM holds). No interaction with redirect (an M-op is never a\n       branch/jump) or load-use (ID/EX is not a load).\n     - On completion EX/MEM captures the M-op with alu_result = mdu result, and\n       rs1_val/rs2_val/write_data taken from the MDU's LATCHED operands, not the\n       live forward mux (while waiting, the producer may have retired to the\n       regfile, making ID/EX.rs?_val stale and the live mux wrong for RVFI).\n     - After completion the M-op result forwards from EX/MEM/MEM-WB exactly\n       like any ALU result; no forwarding-unit change needed.\n\n  4. Tests: add test/test_mdu.py (clocked cocotb) carrying every M-ext vector\n     from test_alu.py (exact bit results incl. div-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1,\n     MULHSU sign cases, back-to-back ops); test_alu.py keeps the RV32I vectors.\n     test_pipeline.py gains a back-to-back dependent MUL->DIV->ADD sequence\n     and an M-op followed by a load-use and a taken branch.\n\n  Expected: Fmax rises from 5.4 MHz to the next-longest path (forward mux ->\n  32-bit adder/comparator -> redirect/PC, or BSRAM->decode->regfile->ID/EX),\n  plausibly 40-70 MHz on GW2AR-18; CoreMark/MHz drops <1% (≈1.6k MULs x 2-3\n  extra cycles + a handful of DIV/REM x ~33 cycles per 449k-cycle iteration).\n  Net fitness several-fold higher than 12.12 (capped at +50 in the schema\n  field below). LUT4 count should also fall well below the 10k target.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mdu.sv\n    description: \"New multi-cycle M-unit: launch/busy/done handshake, operand latches, one shared 33x33 signed DSP multiplier with input+output pipeline regs, radix-2 restoring 32-iteration divider with RV32M div-by-0 / INT_MIN/-1 early-out and sign fix-up, ALTOPS branch with short fixed latency.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Remove all M-ext arms (real and ALTOPS) and the mul_ss/mul_uu/mul_su products; ALU becomes pure RV32I (add/sub/logic/compare/shift/lui).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate mdu; detect valid M-op in ID/EX, launch with post-forward operands, insert EX/MEM bubble while busy, capture mdu result plus latched rs1/rs2 (for rs?_val / write_data) on completion; export mdu_stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add mdu_stall input: stall_if/stall_id hold (no flush) while the M-unit is busy; dmem_stall keeps priority over it; EX/MEM bubble control.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire mdu_stall between ex_stage and hazard_unit; add mdu.sv to the design (source list order unchanged otherwise).\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an is_mdu ctrl bit (or derive it from alu_op range) so EX can steer M-ops without a wide compare.\"\n  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n    description: \"Set is_mdu for funct7=0000001 OP instructions; legality logic unchanged (is_illegal still defaults to 1).\"\n  - file: test/test_mdu.py\n    description: \"New clocked cocotb suite with all M-ext vectors moved from test_alu.py (exact RV32M results incl. div-by-0, INT_MIN/-1, signed/unsigned high halves), start/done handshake and back-to-back launches.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Drop the M-ext vectors (now in test_mdu.py); keep RV32I coverage.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add dependent MUL->DIV->ADD chain, M-op followed by load-use and taken branch, and M-op under dmem stall.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1068, "cache_read_input_tokens": 48899, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1068}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "86e0dbb9-09a3-42f0-badd-d8d71e81fd2a", "uuid": "2272172c-521b-42b9-a0c7-1b9ab7f5cc20", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:05:59.021Z", "request_id": "req_011CfYAyyjEL21J4WkHoykw2"}
