{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYB9eQuGKB7sqZYsEfpr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the hypothesis to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r1s1.yaml`, and it passes the schema check.\n\n**Hypothesis (structural):** take multiply, divide and remainder out of the one-cycle execute stage and give them their own unit (`rtl/mdu.sv`) that takes several cycles and pauses the front of the pipeline while it works.\n\n- **Why:** almost all of the 199.8 ns critical path comes from the four combinational 32-bit dividers in `alu.sv`. CoreMark's timed loop runs almost no divides and only about 1.6k multiplies per iteration, against about 449k cycles per iteration. Making these ops take extra cycles should cost less than 1% in CoreMark/MHz.\n- **Design:**\n  - The divider does one quotient bit per cycle over 32 steps. Divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/−1 are handled up front.\n  - One shared multiplier with pipeline registers replaces the current three.\n  - Under the formal-check (`RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`) build, the unit uses the same handshake with a short fixed delay, so the formal checks still fit their depth limit.\n  - When the result is ready, the source-register values reported for verification (RVFI) come from the operands the unit saved at launch, not the live forwarding mux. While the op waits, the forwarded values can go stale.\n- **Tests:** every exact M-extension test vector moves from `test_alu.py` to a new clocked `test_mdu.py`. `test_pipeline.py` gets dependent-chain, load-use, branch and memory-stall cases.\n\n**Timing probe:** I temporarily replaced only the four divide/remainder ops with zero and ran `make timing TARGET=bench`. The stub gives wrong results, so this measures the timing ceiling, not a working design:\n\n| | Fmax | Logic (LUTs) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | 5.44 MHz | 12,312 |\n| Dividers stubbed | 49.08 MHz | 2,071 |\n\nThat was a single placement option, and I have reverted the change; the worktree is clean. With the dividers gone, the worst paths are all about 25.6 ns: the branch-redirect/stall path is tied with the multiplier path. So the stall signal from the new unit must come only from registered state, or it will lengthen the redirect path and eat into the gain.\n\n**Expected result:** about 2.2 CoreMark/MHz × about 48 MHz ≈ 100 iter/s, roughly 8× the current 12.12. The schema caps the predicted gain at +50%, so the file says 50 with high confidence."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1510,"cache_read_input_tokens":63335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1510},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86e0dbb9-09a3-42f0-badd-d8d71e81fd2a","uuid":"d49c3bcc-71e5-4a82-8a86-5cf6675cd7ab","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:07:05.288Z","request_id":"req_011CfYB9e4KgKCaa7ij9YLSi"}
