{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01W2RdByLTdPMTa1bPRQavoY", "type": "tool_result", "content": "The file /srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-002-r2s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r2s1.yaml has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6a70848a-02fd-4e8b-a210-1ad952381f53", "uuid": "25528003-749d-41e7-9baf-6bc6a19b1eb4", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:27:13.948Z", "tool_use_result": {"filePath": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-002-r2s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r2s1.yaml", "oldString": "  clearing valid bits\". The EX redirect then drives only the PC mux and one\n  ID/EX.valid flop. The branch penalty stays at 2 cycles and no stage is\n  added, so CoreMark IPC is unchanged.", "newString": "  clearing valid bits\". The EX redirect then drives only the PC mux and one\n  ID/EX.valid flop. A taken branch still squashes the same single\n  wrong-path fetch slot, and no stage is added, so CoreMark cycle count is\n  unchanged.", "originalFile": "id: hyp-20260929-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"Valid-bit squash pipeline control: take EX redirect off the fetch/decode/regfile/flush path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion (r1s1, 48.3 MHz, 103.28 iter/s) is limited by a single\n  structural path, not by arithmetic. Timing reports it as regfile LUTRAM /\n  ID/EX -> EX forward mux -> 32-bit branch compare -> redirect -> if_stage NOP\n  mux -> regfile read address + hazard load-use compare -> stall_id/flush_id\n  -> clock-enable / sync-reset of all ~180 ID/EX flops (19 levels, 26.8 ns).\n  The chain exists only because the pipeline squashes wrong-path work by\n  *rewriting data*. A taken branch in EX (1) forces the combinational IF/ID\n  instruction to a NOP. That NOP then feeds the decoder, the regfile read\n  addresses, the regfile write-first bypass compare and the load-use compare.\n  (2) It also drives flush_id, a synchronous reset on every ID/EX bit.\n  stall_id (the ID/EX clock enable) also depends on load_use, which depends\n  on the NOP-muxed rs1/rs2. So the branch compare feeds the regfile read and\n  a high-fanout CE/reset net. None of that is architecturally needed: a\n  squashed instruction only has to be *invalid*, not zero.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change pipeline control from \"flush by zeroing registers\" to \"squash by\n  clearing valid bits\". The EX redirect then drives only the PC mux and one\n  ID/EX.valid flop. The branch penalty stays at 2 cycles and no stage is\n  added, so CoreMark IPC is unchanged.\n\n  1. if_stage: remove the `(flush||redirect) ? NOP : imem_data` mux. out.instr\n     = io_imemData always, and out.valid = imem_ready (the valid bit no longer\n     depends on redirect). The PC register logic stays the same (redirect >\n     stall > pc+4).\n  2. id_stage / reg_file: the decoder, imm_gen, the regfile read addresses and\n     the ID/EX rs?_addr/rd fields all take the raw instruction. The ID/EX\n     register has no reset-on-flush for data fields. Its clock enable is\n     `!(dmem_stall || mdu_stall)`, which depends only on registered state and\n     the stall-gen input, not on redirect or load_use. Only the valid bit sees\n     the squash:\n     valid_d = in.valid && !redirect && !load_use_hazard. On load-use, ID/EX\n     captures a garbage copy of the dependent instruction with valid=0, and\n     the PC holds, so the same instruction is presented again next cycle.\n     That matches today's bubble behaviour.\n  3. hazard_unit: load_use = id_ex.valid && id_ex.mem_read && rd!=0 &&\n     (rd == imem_data[19:15] || rd == imem_data[24:20]), using the raw fetched\n     word. This is safe because (a) redirect and load_use cannot both be true\n     (ID/EX holds either a LOAD or a branch/jump), and redirect still takes\n     priority on the PC, and (b) during an imem stall the IF slot is invalid\n     anyway and the PC holds. A spurious match only re-captures an invalid\n     slot. stall_if = load_use || imem_stall || dmem_stall || mdu_stall\n     (unchanged). flush_id and flush_if are removed.\n  4. ex_stage: qualify every side effect with in.valid at the point it is\n     captured into EX/MEM, not on the critical path. That covers\n     reg_write/mem_read/mem_write/is_illegal/is_mdu in ctrl, and redirect =\n     in.valid && (taken||jump) && !misalign. mdu_stall already uses in.valid.\n     The EX/MEM bubble on mdu_stall can also become valid=0 with ctrl side\n     effects cleared, instead of a full '0 reset. The forward_unit then sees\n     reg_write already qualified by validity. MEM/WB keeps its hold_wb\n     semantics unchanged.\n  5. Optional, only if the post-change timing report still shows the\n     forward-select compare at the head of the path: register the\n     forward-mux selects one cycle early in ID, and hold them on dmem_stall.\n     Select EX/MEM for the new ID/EX when the current ID/EX is valid and\n     writes that rd. Otherwise select MEM/WB when the current EX/MEM writes\n     it. This does not change the data forwarding the EX stage uses. MDU ops\n     still latch operands on their first cycle.\n\n  Expected new worst path: ID/EX -> forward mux -> branch compare -> redirect\n  -> PC mux, or ID/EX -> forward mux -> ALU -> EX/MEM / dmem BSRAM address.\n  Both are about 10-12 levels (about 14-16 ns at the observed ~1.4 ns/level),\n  so roughly 62-70 MHz against 48.3 MHz today, with the same cycle count\n  (4.68 M). The 22%-stall handling is unchanged, and every\n  architectural-state write is still gated by valid bits, so RVFI ordering\n  and trap semantics are preserved.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 28\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Drop the redirect/flush NOP mux on the fetched instruction. out.instr = imem_data, out.valid = imem_ready. The PC update priority (reset > redirect > !stall) is unchanged. Remove the flush input.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Decoder, imm_gen and regfile addresses use the raw instruction. The ID/EX register loses its data reset-on-flush. Data fields load when !hold, where hold = dmem_stall || mdu_stall. valid <= in.valid && !redirect && !load_use when not held, and 0 on reset. Replace the stall/flush ports with hold + squash inputs.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"load_use_hazard is computed from the raw imem_data rs1/rs2 fields and gated by id_ex.valid. Outputs: stall_if (unchanged terms), id_hold = dmem_stall || mdu_stall (no load_use, no redirect), id_squash = load_use || redirect (valid bit only), stall_ex_mem and hold_mem_wb unchanged. Remove flush_if/flush_id.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Gate redirect with in.valid. When capturing EX/MEM, AND ctrl.reg_write/mem_read/mem_write/is_mdu/is_illegal with in.valid, so an invalid ID/EX slot with garbage ctrl cannot write the regfile or memory, forward, or trap. The mdu_stall bubble may clear only valid and the side-effect ctrl bits, not the whole register. Optionally register forward selects (step 5).\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"No functional change required, since reg_write is already valid-qualified at EX/MEM capture. If step 5 is taken, it becomes a next-state select computed from ID/EX + EX/MEM against the raw IF rs1/rs2, registered and held on dmem_stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Rewire the hazard outputs (id_hold, id_squash) into id_stage, and feed raw io_imemData rs fields into the hazard unit. Remove flush wiring to if_stage. No IO or RVFI changes. RVFI still retires on mem_wb.valid.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add directed cases: taken branch whose wrong-path successor is a LOAD/STORE/JAL with garbage fields (must not write memory or regs); load-use immediately after a taken branch target; load-use under imem stall; M-op after a squashed slot; back-to-back taken branches under dmem stall.\"\n", "structuredPatch": [{"oldStart": 19, "oldLines": 8, "newStart": 19, "newLines": 9, "lines": [" hypothesis: |", "   Change pipeline control from \"flush by zeroing registers\" to \"squash by", "   clearing valid bits\". The EX redirect then drives only the PC mux and one", "-  ID/EX.valid flop. The branch penalty stays at 2 cycles and no stage is", "-  added, so CoreMark IPC is unchanged.", "+  ID/EX.valid flop. A taken branch still squashes the same single", "+  wrong-path fetch slot, and no stage is added, so CoreMark cycle count is", "+  unchanged.", " ", "   1. if_stage: remove the `(flush||redirect) ? NOP : imem_data` mux. out.instr", "      = io_imemData always, and out.valid = imem_ready (the valid bit no longer"]}], "userModified": false, "replaceAll": false}}
