{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYCiHJTBQHtatEHPcxUG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the hypothesis to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r2s1.yaml` and it passes the schema check (9 file changes). I didn't build or time it; the Fmax figure below is an estimate.\n\n**Hypothesis: take the EX redirect off the fetch, decode and flush path by squashing with valid bits instead of flushing (category: structural)**\n\n**Why:** The current best core runs at 48.3 MHz, and its slowest path is not arithmetic. It is the path that cancels wrong-path instructions after a taken branch: branch compare → redirect → NOP mux on the fetched instruction → regfile read address and load-use compare → clock enable and reset on all ~180 ID/EX flops. That is 19 logic levels and 26.8 ns. It exists only because cancelled instructions are zeroed. They only need to be marked invalid.\n\n**Proposed change:**\n1. **Fetch:** remove the redirect/flush NOP mux, so the decoder, immediate generator, regfile addresses and load-use check all read the raw fetched word.\n2. **ID/EX register:** its clock enable becomes `!(dmem_stall || mdu_stall)`, which no longer depends on the redirect or on load-use. Only the valid bit is cleared on a squash: `valid <= in.valid && !redirect && !load_use`.\n3. **Hazard unit:** load-use is computed from the raw fetched word and gated by the ID/EX valid bit. It can't clash with a redirect, because the instruction in EX is either a load or a branch, never both. The flush outputs are removed.\n4. **Execute:** the redirect and the writeback, load, store and trap bits are ANDed with the valid bit when they are written into EX/MEM. A cancelled slot holding garbage then can't write registers or memory, forward, or trap.\n5. **Separate address adder:** loads and stores get their own `rs1 + imm` adder with a new `mem_addr` field. Synthesis appears to merge the EX/MEM address register into the data-memory block RAM, so today the whole ALU sits in front of the RAM address. Earlier timing reports end at that RAM, which supports this.\n6. **Optional:** compute the forwarding selects one cycle early and register them, only if the new timing report still starts with the forwarding compare.\n\n**Expected impact: +28%, medium confidence.** The cycle count should stay at about 4.68 M, because a taken branch still cancels one fetch slot and no stage is added. The new slowest path should be about 10–12 levels (forward mux → branch compare → PC mux, or → ALU → EX/MEM). At the ~1.4 ns per level seen so far, that is roughly 62–70 MHz. Even the low end would clear the 4.6% acceptance bar by a wide margin.\n\n**Correctness:** every write to architectural state stays gated by valid bits. Retirement order, trap behaviour, and handling of the ~22% bus stalls don't change. The plan also adds directed tests to `test/test_pipeline.py`:\n- wrong-path loads, stores and jumps with garbage fields\n- load-use right after a branch, and under an instruction-fetch stall\n- a multiply/divide right after a cancelled slot\n- back-to-back taken branches under a data-memory stall"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":305,"cache_read_input_tokens":82673,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":305},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a70848a-02fd-4e8b-a210-1ad952381f53","uuid":"d4c1741d-da4f-457e-873b-068f20c9137d","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:27:37.391Z","request_id":"req_011CfYCiGpurZtcsSZrkaeZt"}
