{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfYDXSQwhSTVupguy1CwB", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01NTtJFnPUhkTCWgi7mp9NM9", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-002-r3s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r3s1.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Operand-latch pipeline: resolve all forwarding + operand selection before ID/EX so EX starts from flops\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion (66.6 MHz, 4.68M cycles) is limited by a 14-level, 20.3 ns path\n  that starts at the register file (cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s) and runs\n  u_id -> u_ex forward mux -> alu_a/alu_b mux -> ALU -> EX/MEM alu_result.\n  The EX stage today does, in series: registered-state rd==rs compares\n  (forward_unit), a 3:1 forward mux, the AUIPC/imm operand mux, the ALU, and\n  the jump/mdu result mux. The regfile read (+ WB write-first bypass) also\n  lands in front of that chain. Everything except the ALU itself can be\n  resolved one stage earlier, in parallel with decode.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the whole bypass network across the ID/EX boundary (\"operand latch\"\n  / result-bus forwarding into the operand registers, as in classic FPGA\n  soft cores):\n  1. In ID, compute forwarding selects from the raw fetched word's rs1/rs2\n     (io_imemData[19:15]/[24:20], a flop in the timed harness) against\n     ID/EX.rd (producer now in EX, valid & reg_write & !mem_read) and\n     EX/MEM.rd (producer now in MEM, reg_write). The WB producer is already\n     covered by the regfile write-first bypass.\n  2. The ID/EX operand registers capture the post-forward values:\n       rs1_fwd = ex_hit1 ? ex_result : mem_hit1 ? mem_result : rf_rs1\n       rs2_fwd likewise\n     where ex_result is the EX-stage writeback value (the same mux that\n     feeds EX/MEM.alu_result: ALU / pc+4 / mdu_result) and mem_result =\n     EX/MEM.mem_to_reg ? load_data (mem_stage's extracted dmem word) :\n     EX/MEM.alu_result. Also latch pre-selected ALU operands\n       op_a = is_auipc ? pc : rs1_fwd,  op_b = alu_src ? imm : rs2_fwd\n     (extra 64 FFs) so the EX ALU input is a bare flop.\n  3. EX then becomes: flops -> ALU -> result mux -> EX/MEM (and -> the ID\n     forward mux). Branch compare, JALR target and the dedicated rs1+imm\n     dmem-address adder read rs1_fwd/rs2_fwd flops directly; store data =\n     rs2_fwd. forward_unit.sv and the EX-side 3:1 muxes are deleted.\n  4. Stall discipline stays as is: load-use still inserts one bubble\n     (consumer immediately behind a LOAD cannot capture until the LOAD is in\n     MEM, where it is forwarded from load_data); id_hold (dmem/mdu stall)\n     simply prevents capture, so the consumer re-evaluates its forward\n     sources the cycle it actually enters EX. When hold is active ID/EX is\n     frozen and already holds final operand values, so the MEM/WB\n     data-retention-for-forwarding special case and the MDU stale-operand\n     latches become unnecessary (may be kept for RVFI simplicity).\n  5. Pin the regfile to distributed LUT-RAM/async read (syn_ramstyle\n     attribute) so its read + write-first bypass completes inside the ID\n     cycle and is registered by the new operand flops, rather than being a\n     synchronous-read RAM whose clock-to-out starts the EX path.\n  Cycle count is unchanged (same forwarding coverage, same 1-cycle\n  load-use bubble, same redirect timing). The EX->EX bypass loop shrinks\n  from ~14 to ~9-10 levels (ALU + result mux + one 3:1 mux), branch\n  compare and the dmem address adder start from flops, and the new ID-side\n  paths (LUTRAM read + bypass + 3:1 + operand mux; dmem -> load extract ->\n  2 mux levels) are each well under the current 20 ns. Expected Fmax\n  ~78-85 MHz at identical IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 18\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add forward-select compares (raw instr rs1/rs2 vs ID/EX.rd and EX/MEM.rd, valid/reg_write/!load gated, x0 excluded), 3:1 operand muxes fed by rf data, ex_result and mem_result, and pre-selected ALU operand muxes (pc/imm). ID/EX captures rs1_fwd, rs2_fwd, op_a, op_b. New inputs: ex_result, ex_rd/ex_fwd_en, mem_result, mem_rd/mem_fwd_en.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Remove fwd_* ports and EX-side forward muxes; ALU reads in.op_a/in.op_b; branch compare, JALR sum, dmem address adder and store data read in.rs1_val/in.rs2_val (now post-forward). Export ex_result (the ALU/pc+4/mdu writeback mux) for ID forwarding. MDU a/b come from ID/EX operands.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Export mem_result = mem_to_reg ? load_data : alu_result (combinational, MEM stage) for ID forwarding. The hold_wb data-retention path is no longer needed for forwarding (keep valid clear for no double retire).\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Delete (or reduce to the ID-side compare helper); no EX-stage forwarding remains.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add op_a/op_b fields to id_ex_t; rs1_val/rs2_val now hold post-forward values.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Rewire: ID gets ex_result/ex rd+enable and mem_result/mem rd+enable; drop forward_unit instance and EX fwd ports. RVFI unchanged (rs?_rdata from the post-forward ID/EX values carried down).\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Add syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed_ram\\\" (LUT-RAM, async read) on regs and drop the per-entry reset loop that blocks clean RAM inference (x0 is already forced to 0 on read), so the read + write-first bypass is fully resolved inside ID and captured by the operand flops.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 590, "cache_read_input_tokens": 65262, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 590}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e2174088-4668-409e-86e3-70e95b3149fc", "uuid": "385f4cab-48cc-4b37-a0a5-e161fb25c423", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:39:28.739Z", "request_id": "req_011CfYDXRvv1Qm2xTdd7EyaH"}
