{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYBGMvfLd4CiCpLiqAZe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the hypothesis to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r1s2.yaml` and it passes the schema check. It proposes zero-bubble branch prediction in the fetch stage, predicting +7% fitness with medium confidence.\n\n**Why this should work:** Fmax is capped at about 5.4 MHz by the combinational divider path (about 200 ns). The predictor logic sits nowhere near that path, so Fmax should stay within place-and-route noise. Any gain in cycles per iteration then carries straight through to fitness.\n\n**What I measured:** I built the exact scored CoreMark binary and ran it through the reference instruction-set simulator, counting only between the start/stop markers. I didn't simulate or synthesize the RTL.\n- 2.92M instructions over the 10 iterations, against about 449k cycles per iteration. That works out to about 1.54 cycles per instruction.\n- Taken branches, JAL and JALR cost 357.5k one-cycle bubbles, about 8% of all cycles.\n\n**How the predictor options compared on that trace:**\n\n| Variant | Bubbles left | Cycles saved |\n|---|---|---|\n| Static backward-taken/forward-not-taken + JAL | 125k | ~5.2% (too close to the 4.6% bar) |\n| 64-entry 2-bit branch history table + JAL | 65k | ~6.5% |\n| **256-entry 2-bit branch history table + JAL (proposed)** | **58k** | **~6.7% (+7.1% throughput)** |\n| 512-entry table | 53.5k | ~6.8% |\n| Adding a return-address stack | –13.7k more | only +0.35%; left out to keep the redirect path simple |\n\nUsing the global-history variant (gshare) instead of a plain table was no better.\n\n**Design:**\n- The fetch stage decodes the incoming instruction and computes the target (pc + immediate) in the same cycle.\n- A 256-entry table of 2-bit counters, indexed by pc[9:2], predicts branch direction. JAL is always predicted taken. Targets that would be misaligned are never predicted.\n- The next PC is chosen in this order: redirect, stall, predicted target, pc+4.\n- The execute stage now redirects only on a misprediction or a taken JALR. A predicted-taken branch or JAL always has the right target, so no 32-bit target compare is needed.\n- The counters are updated from the execute stage, once per branch, even when a memory stall holds it there.\n- The next-PC value recorded for verification is still the one resolved in the execute stage, so the formal checks see the same instruction stream. The counters only affect speed, so leaving them uninitialized can't break correctness.\n\nThe main risk is that the expected gain is about 2.5 points above the 4.6% acceptance bar. A bad place-and-route draw on Fmax could eat that margin."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":274,"cache_read_input_tokens":84940,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":274},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"654a98e9-8783-45a8-8f94-b7abe077f5f3","uuid":"9da31ffa-3bef-4a13-a2dc-3cf417ee487c","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:08:36.970Z","request_id":"req_011CfYBGMUdCijwRXACePZg5"}
