{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfYCapwETLJygUgE8crML", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01HzrwK5WdSDwwCbCWfqCa3Q", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-opus-5_5_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-003-r2s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r2s2.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20260929-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-stage bimodal predictor with a registered (one-cycle-late) mispredict redirect to take branch resolution off the critical path\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion (r1s1, 48.3 MHz, 103.28) is limited by one combinational chain\n  (~25.6-26.8 ns, 17-19 levels): ID/EX -> forward mux -> 32-bit branch compare\n  in EX -> `redirect` -> if_stage NOP mux on out.instr -> regfile read address\n  and hazard_unit load-use compare -> stall/flush of the ID/EX register\n  (endpoint id_ex.rs1_val). The redirect has to be combinational today because\n  the core predicts not-taken: every taken branch/JAL/JALR costs a bubble, and\n  deferring the redirect by one cycle would make that 2 bubbles.\n\n  Measured on the scored binary (r1s2, reference.py trace): 529,588\n  conditional branches (294,425 taken), 49,367 JAL, 13,735 JALR, which is\n  357.5k redirect bubbles out of ~4.68M timed cycles (~7.6%). r1s2's fetch\n  predictor (predecoded pc+imm, 256x2b bimodal, JAL always taken) removed\n  ~298k of them. That puts conditional mispredicts at ~45k, so after the\n  predictor only ~59k control transfers per run (mispredicts + JALR) still\n  need a redirect.\n\n  With a predictor in place, redirects are rare. The redirect can then come\n  from a flop one cycle later and still cost far fewer cycles than today. That\n  removes the EX compare -> IF/hazard/ID-flush chain that sets Fmax.\nhypothesis: |\n  Port the r1s2 fetch predictor to the champion. Resolve mispredicts in EX\n  but act on them from a register in the next cycle, so no EX result\n  combinationally drives the PC, IF/ID, the hazard unit or the ID/EX enables.\n\n  1. Fetch predictor (new rtl/branch_predictor.sv, used by if_stage). Predecode\n     the RAW io_imemData (never the NOP-muxed copy): BRANCH (1100011) and\n     JAL (1101111), B/J immediate, pred_target = pc + imm (one 32-bit adder).\n     JAL is always predicted taken. BRANCH is predicted from a 256 x 2-bit\n     bimodal counter table indexed by pc[9:2] (flops or LUT-RAM, weakly-taken\n     init). Never predict taken when imm[1]=1 (misaligned target); EX's\n     misalign trap then stays unchanged. JALR is not predicted. Carry\n     pred_taken and the 2-bit counter value read at fetch through\n     if_id_t/id_ex_t, so EX computes the saturating update without a second\n     table read port. pred_taken must be 0 whenever out.valid=0.\n     next_pc priority: redirect_q ? ex_mem.pc_next : stall_if ? pc :\n     pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4.\n\n  2. EX resolves, EX/MEM remembers. In ex_stage:\n       actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault\n       mispredict   = in.valid && !kill && (actual_taken != in.pred_taken)\n     No target compare is needed: a predicted-taken branch/JAL used exactly\n     pc+imm, which equals EX's branch_target/jump_target. JALR always\n     mispredicts because pred_taken=0. Register `mispredict` into a new EX/MEM\n     field (only when EX/MEM captures, i.e. !stall_ex_mem && !mdu_stall).\n     The redirect target is the existing EX/MEM.pc_next, which is already the\n     architecturally correct next PC including the misalign fallback, so no\n     new target mux sits in EX. Remove the combinational `redirect` /\n     `redirect_target` outputs.\n\n  3. Kill in the cycle after (kill = redirect_q = EX/MEM.valid &&\n     EX/MEM.mispredict, a flop). Two wrong-path instructions are in flight: W1\n     in ID/EX (being executed) and W2 in fetch.\n       - PC <- EX/MEM.pc_next (overrides every stall, as redirect does today).\n       - ID/EX captures a bubble (flush_id = kill || (load_use && !dmem_stall)).\n       - EX/MEM captures a bubble for W1 (same arm as mdu_stall, reg_q <= '0).\n         Gate W1's mispredict, BHT update and MDU start with !kill, and gate\n         mdu_stall with !kill, so a wrong-path M-op never starts or holds the\n         pipe.\n     A dmem stall cannot coincide with kill: the branch/jump is the\n     instruction in EX/MEM and it has no memory op. So redirect_q is a clean\n     one-cycle pulse. Wrong-path instructions never reach MEM, so they cannot\n     write dmem or the regfile, and RVFI never sees them. The retiring branch\n     already reports pc_wdata = pc_next.\n\n  4. Timing clean-ups that come for free. if_stage stops muxing NOP into\n     out.instr. The regfile read addresses and hazard_unit's load-use rs1/rs2\n     come from raw io_imemData, with validity carried only in the valid bit and\n     the flushes. flush_if becomes imem_stall || kill (all flop-sourced). After\n     this, every PC / ID/EX control input comes from flops plus the short\n     imem_data -> load-use compare. The EX branch compare only feeds EX/MEM D\n     inputs (~9-10 levels, about the same as the fwd -> ALU -> EX/MEM path).\n\n  Expected cycles: bubbles go from 357.5k (1 per taken transfer) to about\n  2 x (45k cond mispredicts + 13.7k JALR) = ~118k. That saves ~240k of\n  ~4.68M cycles, about +5% IPC (r1s2's model matched measurement within\n  0.5 pp). Expected Fmax: the new worst path should be ID/EX -> fwd mux -> ALU\n  -> EX/MEM, or the fetch predecode -> pc+imm adder -> PC mux. Both are\n  roughly 10-12 levels vs today's 17-19, so about 58-63 MHz (+20-30%).\n  Combined estimate is +20-35%, conservatively +22%.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 22\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"New module. Combinational predecode of raw imem data (BRANCH/JAL detect, B/J imm select, pc+imm target adder, no prediction when imm[1]=1, JALR never predicted). 256x2-bit bimodal counter table indexed by pc[9:2], weakly-taken init, read async at fetch. Synchronous update port (en, idx, new 2-bit value) driven from EX. Outputs pred_taken, pred_target, pred_ctr.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor. next_pc = redirect_q ? redirect_pc (EX/MEM.pc_next) : stall ? pc : pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. Remove the NOP mux on out.instr: pass raw imem_data, drive valid = !(imem_stall || kill), and force pred_taken to 0 when !valid. Replace the combinational redirect/redirect_target inputs with the registered kill/redirect_pc.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add pred_taken and pred_ctr[1:0] to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Add a mispredict bit to ex_mem_t.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch pred_taken/pred_ctr into ID/EX. The flush input is now kill || load-use bubble, flop-sourced apart from the short load-use compare. Regfile addresses come from raw instruction bits.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute actual_taken and mispredict = valid && !kill && (actual_taken != pred_taken). Register mispredict in EX/MEM next to the existing pc_next, which serves as the redirect target. Add a kill input that makes EX/MEM capture a bubble (like mdu_stall) and gates mdu start, mdu_stall and the BHT update. Drive the BHT update (en = valid && is_branch && !kill && !stall && !mdu_stall, idx = pc[9:2], saturating inc/dec of the carried pred_ctr by branch_cond). Remove the combinational redirect/redirect_target outputs. Keep the misalign trap and pc_next logic as they are.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the combinational redirect input with the registered kill (EX/MEM.valid && EX/MEM.mispredict). flush_if = imem_stall || kill. flush_id = (load_use || kill) && !dmem_stall (kill and dmem_stall are mutually exclusive by construction). load_use compares ID/EX.rd against the raw fetched rs1/rs2 fields. Everything else is unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire kill/redirect_pc = EX/MEM mispredict/pc_next into if_stage, hazard_unit and ex_stage. Wire the EX BHT update port into if_stage/branch_predictor. Feed hazard/regfile with raw imem rs fields. No IO or RVFI changes; RVFI still retires from MEM/WB.\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: \"Cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: B/J immediates for positive and negative offsets, JAL always predicted, BRANCH follows the counter MSB, counter saturation at 0 and 3, no prediction on imm[1]=1, non-control opcodes and JALR never predicted.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add pipeline cases: correctly predicted taken loop (no bubbles), a mispredicted branch whose wrong-path W1 is a store, an M-op or a load (must not write memory, regfile or start the MDU), a JALR return, a mispredict right after a load that dmem-stalls, and an imem stall in the kill cycle.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 9351, "cache_read_input_tokens": 67631, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 9351}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "203c2930-0815-40a7-902a-b00e2e5935b1", "uuid": "fc140e56-7ed8-4c4f-8a75-b8eec97bb4cb", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:26:18.599Z", "request_id": "req_011CfYCapUhYWLj4iCD6JzMQ"}
