    new target mux sits in EX. Remove the combinational `redirect` /\n     `redirect_target` outputs.\n\n  3. Kill in the cycle after (kill = redirect_q = EX/MEM.valid &&\n     EX/MEM.mispredict, a flop). Two wrong-path instructions are in flight: W1\n     in ID/EX (being executed) and W2 in fetch.\n       - PC <- EX/MEM.pc_next (overrides every stall, as redirect does today).\n       - ID/EX captures a bubble (flush_id = kill || (load_use && !dmem_stall)).\n       - EX/MEM captures a bubble for W1 (same arm as mdu_stall, reg_q <= '0).\n         Gate W1's mispredict, BHT update and MDU start with !kill, and gate\n         mdu_stall with !kill, so a wrong-path M-op never starts or holds the\n         pipe.\n     A dmem stall cannot coincide with kill: the branch/jump is the\n     instruction in EX/MEM and it has no memory op. So redirect_q is a clean\n     one-cycle pulse. Wrong-path instructions never reach MEM, so they cannot\n     write dmem or the regfile, and RVFI never sees them. The retiring branch\n     already reports pc_wdata = pc_next.\n\n  4. Timing clean-ups that come for free. if_stage stops muxing NOP into\n     out.instr. The regfile read addresses and hazard_unit's load-use rs1/rs2\n     come from raw io_imemData, with validity carried only in the valid bit and\n     the flushes. flush_if becomes imem_stall || kill (all flop-sourced). After\n     this, every PC / ID/EX control input comes from flops plus the short\n     imem_data -> load-use compare. The EX branch compare only feeds EX/MEM D\n     inputs (~9-10 levels, about the same as the fwd -> ALU -> EX/MEM path).\n\n  Expected cycles: bubbles go from 357.5k (1 per taken transfer) to about\n  2 x (45k cond mispredicts + 13.7k JALR) = ~118k. That saves ~240k of\n  ~4.68M cycles, about +5% IPC (r1s2's model matched measurement within\n  0.5 pp). Expected Fmax: the new worst path should be ID/EX -> fwd mux -> ALU\n  -> EX/MEM, or the fetch predecode -> pc+imm adder -> PC mux. Both are\n  roughly 10-