{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfYCe1mCG8XyzBVyAUefm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the hypothesis to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r2s2.yaml` and it passes the schema check. I didn't run any simulation or place-and-route for it; the estimates below come from last round's measurements.\n\n**Proposal (predictor category):** add a branch predictor at fetch, and use it to make the branch redirect come from a register one cycle later.\n\n- **Why this angle:** the champion's clock limit (48.3 MHz) is set by one combinational chain. It runs from the branch compare in the execute stage, through the redirect, the fetch-stage no-op mux, the load-use check and the register-file read address, and ends at the execute stage's input register. The redirect has to be instant today only because the core predicts not-taken, so every taken branch or jump costs a cycle. Once a predictor handles most branches, the redirect can safely wait a cycle.\n- **Predictor:** this is the design r1s2 tried last round, now on the champion core.\n  - It decodes the raw fetched instruction and computes the target as pc + immediate.\n  - A 256-entry table of 2-bit counters predicts conditional branches; JAL is always predicted taken; JALR is not predicted.\n  - The counter read at fetch travels with the instruction, so the table needs no second read port for updates.\n- **Registered redirect:**\n  - The execute stage compares the actual outcome with the prediction and stores a mispredict bit in its output register. No target compare is needed.\n  - The correct next PC is already in that register (`pc_next`), so no new target mux is added.\n  - In the following cycle a flop-driven kill signal sets the PC and turns the two wrong-path instructions into bubbles. It also stops a wrong-path multiply/divide from starting or stalling the pipeline.\n  - A data-memory stall can't coincide with the kill, because the instruction in the memory stage at that point is the branch itself, which doesn't access memory.\n- **Other clean-up:** the fetch stage stops forcing a no-op into the instruction on a flush. The register-file addresses and load-use check read the raw fetched instruction, and a flushed instruction is marked by its valid bit alone.\n\n**Expected impact: +22%, medium confidence.**\n- **Cycles:** stall cycles from branches and jumps drop from about 357.5k to about 118k. That is about 2 per mispredict or JALR, from roughly 45k mispredicts plus 13.7k JALRs. It works out to about +5% instructions per cycle. Last round the same trace model predicted r1s2's result to within 0.5 points.\n- **Clock:** the longest path should drop from 17–19 logic levels to about 10–12. It would then be either the execute-stage ALU path or the fetch path through the target adder to the PC, giving roughly 58–63 MHz.\n\n**Main risks:** a wrong-path instruction must never write memory or registers or start the multiply/divide unit. And the fetch-stage predecode, adder and PC mux must stay shorter than the execute-stage path, otherwise the clock gain is smaller than estimated."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":954,"cache_read_input_tokens":80879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":954},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"203c2930-0815-40a7-902a-b00e2e5935b1","uuid":"b1845332-f9db-4caf-9ab4-8d45851faa48","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:26:38.897Z","request_id":"req_011CfYCe1Jf3ti2uVSWGdUzg"}
