{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_015cntiQ2SeohQG2uq79zgfr", "type": "tool_result", "content": "id : \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\"\ntitle : \"Zero-bubble fetch-stage branch prediction: predecode + 256-entry bimodal BHT, predicted JAL\"\ncategory : \"predictor\"\nmotivation : \"Fmax is pinned at ~5.4 MHz by the combinational divider path\\n(fwd -> EX -> ALU, ~200 ns). Logic outside that path does not affect\\nFmax, so this hypothesis can only move fitness through IPC, and any\\nIPC gain maps 1:1 onto fitness.\\n\\nEvery taken control transfer currently costs one fetch slot. Control\\nflow resolves in EX; the instruction fetched behind it (pc+4) is\\nflushed and the target is fetched one cycle later. I measured this on\\nthe exact scored binary (-O3, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, ITERATIONS=10)\\nwith test/cosim/reference.py, between the 0x10000100 and 0x10000104\\nmarkers:\\n  - 2,922,016 retired instructions (~292k per iteration). The champion\\n    spends 5.442e6/12.12 = ~449k cycles per iteration (CPI ~1.54).\\n  - 529,588 conditional branches (294,425 taken; 235,191 of the taken\\n    ones are backward), 49,367 JAL, 13,735 JALR (all returns).\\n  - That is 357,527 redirect bubbles, ~35.8k per iteration = ~8.0% of\\n    all cycles.\\nBecause IF/ID is combinational (the instruction is decoded in the same\\ncycle it is fetched), a predictor placed there can steer the very next\\nfetch and remove the bubble completely for correct predictions.\\n\"\nhypothesis : {\"motivation\": \"Fmax is pinned at ~5.4 MHz by the combinational divider path\\n(fwd -> EX -> ALU, ~200 ns). Logic outside that path does not affect\\nFmax, so this hypothesis can only move fitness through IPC, and any\\nIPC gain maps 1:1 onto fitness.\\n\\nEvery taken control transfer currently costs one fetch slot. Control\\nflow resolves in EX; the instruction fetched behind it (pc+4) is\\nflushed and the target is fetched one cycle later. I measured this on\\nthe exact scored binary (-O3, TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, ITERATIONS=10)\\nwith test/cosim/reference.py, between the 0x10000100 and 0x10000104\\nmarkers:\\n  - 2,922,016 retired instructions (~292k per iteration). The champion\\n    spends 5.442e6/12.12 \n…[21874 of 24946 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae37e5759e9c2f78]…\nbuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}\nlogic_levels : 16\nformal_passed : true\ncosim_passed : true\nerror : null\nimplementation_notes : \"\"\ntimestamp : \"2026-09-29T18:34:50.137341Z\"\nslot : 2\nround_id : 2\nlesson : \"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, +26.66%): Adding the r1s2 fetch predictor (predecode, pc+imm adder, 256x2b BHT) and acting on a registered EX/MEM.mispredict one cycle late did take the EX branch compare off the Fmax path, but it lost to sibling r2s1's valid-bit squash (+37.69%, 66.6 MHz at unchanged cycles); since fitness = 10*Fmax/Mcycles and cycles must fall between r1s2's 4.19M and the champion's 4.68M, r2s2 can only have reached ~55-61 MHz, so the new fetch predecode -> pc+imm -> next_pc path and/or the flush_id zeroing of ID/EX on load-use it kept now caps Fmax well below r2s1, and the ~5% IPC from prediction does not make up for that; any predictor added on top of r2s1 must keep its fetch -> PC path shorter than r2s1's ~15 ns EX -> ALU -> EX/MEM path.\"\n======", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b141ed81-537f-4d6c-99e8-fe2d72835635", "uuid": "22d054ac-3db1-4c0b-9bbe-e76c924e3a8b", "timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:39:19.131Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "id : \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\"\ntitle : \"Zero-bubble fetch-stage branch prediction: predecode + 256-entry bimodal BHT, predicted JAL\"\ncategory : \"predictor\"\nmotivation : \"Fmax is pinned at ~5.4 MHz by the combinational divider path\\n(fwd -> EX -> ALU, ~200 ns). Logic outside that path does not affect\\nFmax, so this hypothesis can only move fitness through IPC, and any\\nIPC gain maps 1: